	abstract = {The rapid aging of global populations necessitates innovative aged care solutions, with smart service platforms emerging as a promising approach. However, data silos, high costs, and weak user engagement hinder their sustainable development, particularly in China where government-led platforms dominate through public-private partnerships. Hence, the study aims to investigate the influencing factors of sustainable development and identify optimal strategies to balance data utility with privacy protection. The study employs a novel mixed-methods approach combining case analysis of three regional platforms with multi-agent simulation modeling. The case studies identified 20 critical sustainability factors using the TOUE (Technology-Organization-User-Environment) framework, including data governance, service innovation, information literacy, policy support, etc. The multi-agent model simulates interactions among governments, platforms, providers, and recipients under varying data-sharing scenarios. Simulation results indicate that the sustainable development of aged care platforms requires a delicate equilibrium - where data sharing serves as both a catalyst for service innovation and a responsibility demanding governance. The study also reveals the diminishing returns of excessive data security investments, suggesting an optimal threshold for sustainable operations. The study makes theoretical contributions by advancing the complex adaptive systems theory in the context of aged care ecosystems and also contributes to the discourse on sustainable digital transformation based on insights from China's rapidly aging context, with implications for public sector datafication and optimizing data sharing strategies in public-private partnership models. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd.},
