	abstract = {Health care is being reshaped by digital transformation, demographic shifts, climate risk, and widening inequities. Nurse leaders must increasingly navigate ethical complexity, lead innovation, and anticipate future disruptions. At the same time, the 2021 AACN Essentials and the AONL Nurse Leader Core Competencies call for competency-based preparation of nurses as leaders at all levels. This article links these expectations to practical actions that education–practice partners can take to develop future-focused nurse leaders. We propose 3 interrelated strands of leadership formation: innovation as a core literacy, not a niche role; ethics and moral resilience as central leadership capacities; and foresight leadership and futures literacy as disciplines for shaping, not merely reacting to, change. Using examples from design thinking, simulation, ethics and moral resilience work, and futures methods, the article illustrates how clinical settings can become learning laboratories where students, new graduates, and emerging leaders practice these capabilities in real-time. A skimmable action table outlines specific steps nurse leaders can take with academic partners, including aligning frameworks, codefining shared leadership outcomes, opening leadership forums to learners, sponsoring innovation and foresight exercises, and embedding ethics reflection into existing structures. Together, these actions help build a pipeline of nurses who are prepared to innovate, act with integrity under pressure, and lead organizations through uncertain futures. © 2025 Elsevier Inc.},
