	abstract = {In the digital era, corporate digital transformation has become a crucial strategy for enhancing competitive advantage. This study investigates the relationship between supplier concentration and corporate digital transformation using a hybrid methodology of Ordinary Least Squares regression and multiple machine learning techniques. Our analysis reveals that higher supplier concentration impedes corporate digital transformation, with more pronounced negative effects on relatively smaller listed firms, state-owned enterprises, high-tech firms, and non-consumer sectors. We find that supplier concentration primarily obstructs digital transformation by diminishing a firm's negotiating power, elevating operational risks, and increasing financing costs. Furthermore, our results identify a significant spillover effect: a firm’s high supplier concentration also inhibits the digital transformation of its suppliers. This research provides new evidence on the barriers to corporate digital transformation and contributes to methodological exploration in digital economy research. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
