	abstract = {This article examines the story of ABILITY Magazine, founded by Chet Cooper in 1990 as a pioneering platform for disability representation in the media. Drawing from a series of extensive interviews with Cooper, it explores the magazine’s challenges in securing national distribution, advertising revenue, and mainstream visibility while resisting reductive narratives of disability as either tragedy or inspiration. Through celebrity features, corporate partnerships, and digital innovations, ABILITY has positioned itself at the intersection of advocacy, business, entertainment, and inclusion. This oral history seeks to address the disability perspective gap in media history literature by showcasing the story of one of the longest-running disability magazines in the world. As digital media reshape traditional publishing models, ABILITY remains a case study in persistence and adaptability, illustrating how disability-focused media can challenge stereotypes and carve out sustainable, influential spaces within the broader media industry. © 2025 The Author(s). Published with license by Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.},
