	abstract = {This viewpoint analyses the critical gap between Australian construction education and industry digital requirements, proposing evidence-based collaborative frameworks to develop workforce capabilities. Drawing on systematic reviews and international case studies, the analysis reveals that Australian programs lack structured industry partnerships, industrial doctorate pathways, and integrated digital pedagogies that characterise successful European models. The viewpoint presents three strategic interventions: (1) establishing industrial doctorate programs where students serve as boundary spanners between academia and industry; (2) implementing BIM-integrated collaborative learning environments that demonstrably enhance graduate employment outcomes; and (3) developing policy frameworks supporting micro-credentials and flexible qualification pathways. The complementarity investment principle guides resource allocation, where universities provide pedagogical expertise while industry contributes practical context and infrastructure. Implementation strategies differentiate between research-intensive universities, teaching-focused institutions, and vocational providers, ensuring contextually appropriate approaches. The framework addresses equity challenges in digital access while recognising regional disparities. Without immediate adoption of these collaborative mechanisms, Australia’s construction sector faces continued productivity decline and diminished international competitiveness in an increasingly digitalised global market. © The Author(s) 2025},
