	abstract = {This study explores how digital transformation revitalises the urban informal economy in Makassar, Indonesia, with a particular focus on gender role dynamics. It investigates how technology adoption reshapes economic activities and contributes to gender transformation in Asia’s fast-urbanizing contexts. A qualitative grounded theory approach was applied. Data were collected through in-depth interviews, participant observations, and secondary documentation from the Central Bureau of Statistics of South Sulawesi. The research site, Ir. Hertasning Street in Makassar, represents a hub of diverse informal traders ranging from small fruit vendors to large-scale secondhand goods dealers. Results indicate that digitalization particularly through mobile phones and online transaction platforms has become essential for competitiveness, enhancing market access, operational efficiency, and customer trust. The study reveals notable gender transformations, with women increasingly entering business spaces traditionally dominated by men. This shift is evident in mobility patterns, types of businesses managed, and renegotiated household roles. The findings highlight the economic resilience of the informal sector and its symbiotic relationship with the formal economy. Policymakers in Asia can leverage these insights to design inclusive urban economic strategies that integrate digital infrastructure, provide digital literacy programs, and extend social protection schemes to informal workers, thereby supporting sustainable growth. This research provides Asia-specific evidence on how digitalization not only strengthens informal sector competitiveness but also accelerates gender role transformation. It contributes to regional debates on urban economic development, showcasing the informal sector’s role as a critical driver of inclusive growth in developing Asian cities. © 2025, Green Publication. All rights reserved.},
