	abstract = {Shaping the low-carbon resilience of small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) is becoming increasingly important for promoting high-quality economic growth. This study leverages data from A-share listed companies in Shanghai and Shenzhen from 2016 to 2023 to examine the relationship between the “Specialized, Refinement, Differential and Innovation” (SRDI) qualification accreditation policy and the low-carbon resilience of SMEs utilizing a multi-temporal double difference method. The findings indicate that the SRDI qualification accreditation policy enhances SMEs’ low-carbon resilience, with more pronounced effects on non-state-owned firms and polluting SMEs. Mechanistic analyses suggest that the recognition tactics under the SRDI qualification accreditation may bolster SMEs’ low-carbon resilience through digital transformation, government subsidies, industry-academia partnerships, and synergistic clustering mechanisms within industries. These results provide valuable insights and implications for global SMEs to adopt chain thinking, improve low-carbon resilience, and optimize their innovation ecosystems. © 2025 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.},
