	abstract = {Digital technologies can completely transform the manufacturing supply chain and satisfy the dynamic requirements of the market. Developing countries like India face huge challenges around Industry 4.0 technology adoption. While academicians and researchers have focused on either standalone firms or a specific Industry 4.0 technology, a broader technology adoption across manufacturing supply chains remains relatively unexplored. This paper emphasizes on identifying and assessing the barriers of Industry 4.0 technology adoption across manufacturing supply chain. This paper idntified 12 significant barriers for digital transformation in the manufacturing supply chain. The global weight of every barrier and sub-barrier is analysed and evaluated using the SWARA approach and interpretive structural modeling. Dependent, Linkage, Driving, and Autonomous barriers are further categories for barriers. Government initiatives and industry-friendly regulations that address data security concerns and the availability of skilled talent for the effective execution of integrated technologies can accelerate digital transformation. The focus should first be placed on the following issues: lack of maturity and readiness of the partner ecosystem (PE), financial challenges (FC), lack of management support (MS), and lack of regulatory support and legal legislation (RS), lack of standardization for technical collaboration (STC), and lack of data security (DS). Furthermore, the SWARA analysis reveals significant barriers to data sharing, including lack of standardized protocols, lack of manager willingness, a culture that discourages innovation, and insufficient workforce knowledge and skills. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
